    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00001",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00124:title:sect-00001-title",
        "text": "Steric Effects in the S[[sub:N]]2 Reaction"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00003",
          "segmentId": "m00124:para:para-00003"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00002",
          "caption": {
            "segmentId": "m00124:caption:fig-00002-caption"
          },
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00124:alt:fig-00002-alt",
              "text": "The figure shows the structures and space-filling models of four compounds. The substrate carbon is more accessible to nucleophilic attack (less hindered) in methyl bromide and most hindered in t-butylbromide."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_11_03_002.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00004",
          "segmentId": "m00124:para:para-00004"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00003",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00124:alt:fig-00003-alt",
              "text": "A reaction between R-Br and chloride ion gives R-Cl and bromide ion. S N 2 reactivity of five compounds are in increasing order: Tertiary, Neopentyl, Secondary, Primary, and Methyl."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_11_03_003.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00005",
          "segmentId": "m00124:para:para-00005"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00004",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00124:alt:fig-00004-alt",
              "text": "Structures of vinylic halide and aryl halide show such molecules cannot react with a nucleophile because there is no clear path of attack on the backside of the carbon."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_11_03_004.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
